  "digital-health-ai-hot-takes-2": "Absolutely. I'm Turkey. Uh\n\nmy name is Joseph Matary.\n\nI'm Sakar from Indonesia.\n\nMy name is Isma. Okay. My name is Tim. My name is Ronaldo.\n\nMy name is Nati.\n\nI'm Dr. Aral Surmeli. I'm Arwin.\n\nMy name is Rob.\n\nMy name is Sam Woo.\n\nSo my name is Dick Martin.\n\nI'm Liz Nad.\n\nMy hot take.\n\nMy hot take is\n\nSo this is my hot take.\n\nAnd my hot take is\n\nSo my hot take is\n\nAnd my hot take is here's my hot take.\n\nMy hot take.\n\nHere's my hot take.\n\nAbsolutely sizzling hot take. GDHF global perspectives on digital health podcast. It is time for the hot takes. Oh my god, that is a hot take.\n\nHi, my name is Shant Padi, host of the global perspectives on digital health podcast. Hot takes video number two. My hot take here at the global digital health forum this year is where's the money coming from? We're talking a lot about new innovative technologies. We're talking about new ways to avoid inequities within technology, but really the reality is now who's funding it? Where's the money going to come from? How do we make sure that we don't repeal all the progress that's already been made? How do we make sure that we can still sustain the foundations especially in governments, especially in the enabling environment in the ecosystem that makes digital technology thrive and actually be equitable?\n\nThe one thing I guess I'm really pleased to see this year is the um the failures session. Um I think last year I came away quite frustrated that this sort of seems to be the Instagram of uh of sort of digital conferences where everybody's doing wonderful things. Nobody talks about failures. Nobody's honest about it. And you know I think there needs to be sort of a bit of a shift in people's transparency around what we're trying to do. We're deploying complex um complex digital solutions in really difficult environments and for people not to talk openly or not feel safe to speak openly about their failures I think is a real missed opportunity.\n\nSo um yeah, I'm excited for that um that failure session and uh and hopefully people can be super honest and uh it'll be a lot better for everybody's mental health cuz um I think yeah, Instagram's very bad for people's mental health. These these conferences can be as well. We are a podcast that shares the insights of people who are creating impact for underserved communities around the world. Policy, research, regulation, innovators, implementers, and we've been roving around the conference to take people's hot takes about what still needs to happen. Just on my way to day two of the global digital health forum here in Nairobi. Let's do this. that AI digital solutions will either succeed or fail based on the community health workers themselves and not your high resource hospital settings.\n\nReason for that is our community healthcare workers are the absolute backbone of a lot of the healthare systems across the world but specifically in Africa and without them their buying their engagement and their trust AI digital solutions will not be as successful as they can be. Um, in this 2025 global digital health forum, there's a lot of talk and hype about AI, but we should be careful not to overindex AI and forget about uh what they are meant to achieve in improving health outcomes. Let's not also forget the health workers. People still have a significant role to play in or within the health sector.\n\nA lot of tools being developed for all things AI. The velocity is really incredible. Um, what we don't have enough of are really high quality trainings to keep people up to date with these tools and build a core set of competencies and skills so that they're able to keep up with the madness and the velocity of all of these tools that are coming out. To me, it's all about more training, meeting people where they are, and building communities around that training so that we're able to investigate and analyze and build those competencies together in community. My hot take is we have such an opportunity now for everything to change. And we have an opportunity to remember something that I think we forget sometimes along the way. When it comes to international development, we must always be thinking about how we work ourselves out of a job.\n\nAnd as soon as we work ourselves out of a job, there will be far more jobs to do.\n\nAnd my hot take is that the main VCs have to start considering the social med startups which are actually creating both business and impact value. The global perspectives on digital health podcast is all about sharing learning across contexts around the world on what it takes to create impact in underserved communities. Stay tuned. Share. Subscribe, listen, watch.\n\nJust Hey, it's crazy times out there. Keep the faith. Do the right thing. That is awesome.",
